# UI and UX

- Calm developer-tool look: neutral surfaces, one accent (`primary`), status
  colours only for status (`success`, `warning`, `danger`).
- Colours come from the tokens in `web/src/index.css` (`bg-card`,
  `text-muted-foreground`, `border`...). No raw hex or Tailwind palette colours
  in components. Both light and dark must read well; check both.
- Components follow shadcn/ui patterns: `cva` variants, `cn()` for classes,
  files in `web/src/components/ui/`.
- Every icon-only button has an accessible name (`sr-only` text or
  `aria-label`). Status icons have labels. Errors use `role="alert"`.
- Long jobs show progress steps and keep the output one click away. Never a
  blocking modal during a run.
- Destructive or risky actions ask inline before running, with the fix's
  `confirm` text.
- Layouts work from 375 px wide: stack on small screens, no horizontal scroll.
  A grid that holds cards gets `grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)]` (before
  `lg:grid-cols-2`), so a long name in a card is cut with an ellipsis instead
  of pushing the card past its column.
- Keyboard first: visible focus, Enter submits, actions reachable by Tab.

## One pattern on every page

- **A job shows where it was started**: as the footer of the card whose
  button started it (`JobFooter`, or `JobBox` under a `border-t` line, in
  `web/src/components/job-box.tsx`), scrolled into view, with its steps,
  Retry when it failed and the link to Undo once it settled. Never a box or
  card of its own between cards: the cards around it stay where they are. A
  job started from a row's menu in a long list shows at the top of that list,
  inside its card. The header's jobs indicator is the view of every job; a
  page never adds a second place for the same job.
- **Changes you can undo is the last card of its view**, full width, and only
  there while something can be undone (`CleanupsCard`, `RowChangesCard`): at
  the bottom it never pushes another card when it appears. The toast after a
  change has its own Undo as well.
- **A form with several fields saves through `SaveBar`**: it sticks to the
  bottom of the window while there are changes ("2 unsaved changes",
  Discard, and the one button that saves or applies, named for what it does:
  "Save and restart Apache", "Apply"). A single switch or button acts at once.
- **Every card that changes something says what it applies to** with
  `ScopeLabel` in its header: "all sites", "PHP 8.3", "this site".
- **An inherited value always shows its number and where it comes from**:
  "Inherit: 128M (php.ini)", "Inherit: 2M (PHP's built-in default)". Never a
  bare "default".
- **Global things sit above per-item things.** A page about many items (PHP
  versions, databases) starts with what applies to all of them, then the item
  you picked, under its own heading.
- **Tabs come in groups and keep their place.** Related tabs sit together
  (a site: Overview and Logs; WordPress and its data; the server) with a line
  between groups, and a tab an item lacks leaves the rest where they are. A
  tab's badge counts only what needs a look, colored by how bad it is, never
  how many things the tab lists. An Overview shows a fact from each other tab
  and opens it; a setting lives in its tab, next to what it changes.
- **One word for one thing.** A site with a PHP version or settings of its
  own has its "own PHP"; the version every other site runs is "the default".
- **Confirm dialogs**: the title asks the question, the description says
  what happens and what Undo does, Cancel on the left, the action named for
  what it does on the right.
- **Secrets use `SecretInput`, never a password field.** A sudo password, an
  API key, a site's or a database's password is text drawn as dots with every
  password manager's ignore marker (`web/src/lib/secret-field.ts`), so the
  browser never offers to save it. Only the panel login and the panel
  password use `PasswordInput`; `web/test/secret-field.test.ts` keeps it so.

## Long lists in cards

A list inside a summary card (the WordPress view's options, meta keys, tables,
issues, users, clean-up kinds) shows its first `WP_DB_LIST_ROWS` (7) rows and
a **Show all N** link under it, remembered per site and list in this
browser (`dmp-wpdb-show-all` in localStorage), so another site opens short. When the rows it hides can still be acted on (ticked
clean-up kinds), the link says how many. A name or a count that has rows
behind it opens them in Tables: plain filters or a clean-up's kind go in the
history entry (`navigate(..., { state })`), never the address.

## One layout per page type

Every page is made of the pieces in `web/src/components/page.tsx` and
`web/src/components/skeletons.tsx`, and follows the layout of its type.

- **The pieces.** `PageHeader` (title and intro left, actions right, Check
  again last) tops a page; `ViewBar` (a picker or filters left, Check again
  right, "Read 2 min ago" and a short note under it) tops a tab or a view.
  The button that reads a page again is always `CheckAgain`: never Refresh or
  Read again. Something the whole page or view should know is a `Notice` right
  under its header or bar (danger, warning, info); a field's own error stays
  next to the field. Nothing yet is an `EmptyState`: what would be here and
  the way to get it.
- **Loading shows the page's shape**, never a blank box or a "Reading..." line
  alone: a skeleton of the real layout with a `Reading` line that says what is
  read (`CardsSkeleton`, `ListSkeleton`, `TableSkeleton`, `LogSkeleton`, and
  `ReadingBlock` inside a card). A part read after the page (a tile, a card)
  keeps its place and fills in.
- **List pages** (Sites, Jobs, Run history, Archive, WordPress cores, Mail,
  Aura): header, notices, a filter bar, then one card of rows. Loading is a
  list or table skeleton; nothing is an empty state.
- **Detail pages** (a site, a job, a run): back link, title and facts,
  actions, then tabs, Overview first.
- **Status pages** (Environment, Developer options, Updates, Services and its
  Apache, nginx and Cron tabs): header with Check again, notices, the summary
  first, then **pairs then full width**: short summary cards side by side,
  long lists and logs across the whole width at the bottom.
- **Tool pages** (a site's Database: WordPress, Backups and tools; Services,
  MySQL): a `ViewBar`, then **two independent columns** of cards, each card one
  task with its job as its footer, and Changes you can undo last at full
  width.
- **Settings pages** (a site's PHP and Config, Services, PHP per version, cron
  tasks): one column of cards, each with its `ScopeLabel`, a `SaveBar` while
  there are changes, a job in the card it changes.
- **Data pages** (Tables, a table's rows and structure, SQL): a bar, one large
  card with the table and its paging, then Undo last.
- **Flows** (New site and its steps, login, an update's install) keep their
  own step layout.
